                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Better than Angry Birds!  \n\nDoes Angry Birds have siege engines that you can upgrade?  No.  Does Angry Birds have mages that cast spells to destroy one or more of your siege engines?  No.\n\nYes this game has the same basic ideas as Angry Birds:  launch a projectile to hit all the enemies in a set number of turns.  But Angry Birds was not the first game to do that, so why do people disregard games like this just because they are similar?  Who cares which was first, all that matters is if a game is good or not.  Sure there are lots of crappy games that are made to try to get easy money, but dont' assume that this is one of them.  \n\nFor one thing, this game has no animals.  No birds, no pigs.  The player has siege engines such as catapults and arrow launchers.  The enemies are mages of different types.  If you don't kill them in a turn or two the mages cast a spell and destroy one or more of your siege engines.   And the mages don't laugh and mock you for losing like the pigs in Angry Birds.\n\nThere are 4 different types of siege engines you get throughout the game.  You can't choose which to use, they are set in each level.  But you can choose which ones you want to upgrade.   You get upgrade points (I can't remember what they are called in the game) from time to time (I think for beating a level fast or maybe not losing a siege engine or something) and there are occasional orbs in the level that you can get, though they are usually in hard to reach places.\n\nThere are also spells you can use.  I've gotten one at the beginning of each world.  Once you use it, it's gone.  But it does help when you're in a tough spot.\n\nGraphics are nice, cartoony, cute.\n\nSound is ok.\n\nThe price is great.  Pretty cheap for the amount of content you get.  \n\nPlus there are Achievements and Trading Cards!",
